PLACES=[
# ---- The South Branch: Batoche, Fish Creek, St. Laurent, Fort Carlton ----
N('batoche-nhs','Batoche: St.-Antoine-de-Padoue church, rectory and village site','Batoche','sk','town','standing',52.75237,-106.11754,'exact','public','walk','','1872–1885',
  'The Métis village of Batoche grew up from 1872 where the Carlton Trail crossed the South Saskatchewan River, with river lots running back from the water. It was the seat of Louis Riel\'s provisional government in 1885 and the scene of the last battle of that conflict, in May 1885, when Middleton\'s Field Force defeated the Métis. The village site was abandoned by about 1920; the parish church and rectory of St.-Antoine-de-Padoue, built by the Oblates before 1885, still stand, and the place has been a national historic site since 1923.',
  'The church and rectory are Red River frame buildings (squared timbers set into grooved posts), restored by Parks Canada; the register also names the dovetailed-log Caron House, the cemetery, rifle pits and the zareba (the Field Force\'s entrenched camp) on the battlefield, traces of the Carlton Trail and the telegraph cut line, and Dominion survey mounds. The Museums Association listing says guided tours run daily in summer; interior fittings not checked.',
  'The Métis capital of 1885, where the church that was shelled still stands.',
  'Parks Canada national historic site, 88 km northeast of Saskatoon (1040 R.R.1, Wakaw; 306-423-6227). The Museums Association listing gives May 15 to September 15, 9 a.m. to 5 p.m., with days varying in May and September (call ahead); Parks Canada\'s own hours page could not be read here, though it said the site was closed for the season when looked at in November 2026. Fees not checked.',
  [W('Batoche'),HP('Batoche National Historic Site of Canada',14598),MAS_B,PC_B],th=[52.75419, -106.11024],thn='Batoche visitor centre lot (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no'),
N('tourond-coulee-fish-creek','Battle of Tourond\'s Coulee / Fish Creek (Tourond homestead)','Fish Creek','sk','fort','traces',52.53901,-106.152659,'site','public','walk','','1885',
  'On April 24, 1885 Gabriel Dumont\'s Métis, with Cree and Dakota allies from the Beardy and One Arrow First Nations, waited in Tourond\'s Coulee, a ravine off the trail along Fish Creek, for Middleton\'s North West Field Force marching north to Batoche. The register gives 10 soldiers killed and about 45 wounded against 6 Métis dead; the Tourond homestead was destroyed by shellfire. Middleton camped near the field for two weeks before moving on Batoche.',
  'A 36-hectare federal site on the banks of Fish Creek: the coulee, the creek bed and high banks, and the cellar and foundations of Mme Josephte Tourond\'s homestead, with traces of the trail to the river crossing (Canadian Register).',
  'The Métis ambush that stopped Middleton for two weeks.',
  'Parks Canada national historic site, managed from Batoche (306-423-6227); Parks Canada says to see Batoche\'s hours. Parks Canada\'s page (modified August 13, 2026): the south access is closed by a washout and the site is reached from the north.',
  [W('Battle of Fish Creek'),HP('Battle of Tourond\'s Coulee / Fish Creek NHS',14085),PC_T],brick='?'),
N('st-laurent-shrine','St. Laurent Shrine (Our Lady of Lourdes) and the 1883 rectory','St. Laurent de Grandin','sk','civic','standing',52.85042,-106.09419,'site','public','drive','','1873–1883',
  'Father Alexis André and Métis families from Manitoba founded the mission of St. Laurent in 1873, with a church, a residential school and a convent. A priest likened the hillside spring to Lourdes in 1879; a shrine was set up in 1881, a grotto in 1882, and a statue given in 1885 after the first reported cure, and the first formal pilgrimage came in 1905. The mission closed in 1894 after the upheaval of 1885; the rectory of about 1883 is the last building from the mission years.',
  'On a 32-hectare property beside the South Saskatchewan: the wood-frame rectory (about 1883), the grotto (rebuilt many times, last in 1951), the 1885 statue, and a cemetery with graves of local people killed in 1885. The log church of 1938 burned in 1990; the church there now is a replica of it (Canadian Register). Interior not checked.',
  'A Métis mission\'s spring that drew pilgrims from 1905 on.',
  'A Catholic shrine and municipal heritage property of the RM of Duck Lake, about 11 km northeast of Duck Lake. The town of Duck Lake\'s visitor page mentions a picnic area and grotto and two pilgrimages a year. Hours not checked.',
  [W('St. Laurent de Grandin'),HP('St. Laurent Shrine',11530),L('Town of Duck Lake: St. Laurent Shrine','https://ducklake.ca/visitors/st_laurent_shrine.html')],brick='no'),
N('fort-carlton-sk','Fort Carlton (Carlton House) Provincial Historic Park','Duck Lake','sk','fort','standing',52.86978,-106.52987,'site','public','drive','','1810–1885',
  'The Hudson\'s Bay Company\'s Carlton House on the North Saskatchewan, on the Carlton Trail, was the company\'s main source of pemmican and a crossroads of river and cart routes from 1810. Treaty 6 was signed near it on August 23, 1876. Crozier fell back here after the Battle of Duck Lake in March 1885 and abandoned it two days later; a fire started in the evacuation destroyed the fort, and Big Bear surrendered near the ruins that July. The province rebuilt part of the post in 1967.',
  'A reconstructed stockade and replica buildings of the late 1870s: Wikipedia says four, of Red River frame, the Canadian Register says five (Tourism Saskatchewan names a fur and provisions store, trade store and clerk\'s quarters), standing over the archaeological remains of the real post, with traces of the Carlton Trail, the fort\'s garden plot and maple groves (Canadian Register). These are replicas, not survivors.',
  'Where Treaty 6 was signed and where the fort burned in 1885.',
  'A Saskatchewan provincial historic park: Tourism Saskatchewan says 26 km west of Duck Lake on Highway 212, about 100 km north of Saskatoon by Highways 11 and 212, with hiking trails, a picnic area and a campground (306-270-2436). Sask Parks\' own page is drawn by script and could not be read: hours and fees not checked.',
  [W('Fort Carlton'),HP('Fort Carlton Provincial Park',4168),L('Tourism Saskatchewan: Fort Carlton Provincial Historic Park','https://tourismsaskatchewan.com/provincialpark/1809/fort-carlton-provincial-park')],th=[52.87015, -106.52775],thn='Fort Carlton park lot off Highway 212 (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no'),
# ---- Rosthern and Blaine Lake ----
N('seager-wheeler-farm','Seager Wheeler\'s Maple Grove Farm','Rosthern','sk','industry','standing',52.67370,-106.21891,'site','public','drive','','1898–1928',
  'Seager Wheeler, the "Wheat King", farmed here from 1898 to 1947 and bred wheat and fruit strains for the prairie climate; wheat grown here won world championships five times between 1911 and 1918 (Wikipedia). He put up most of the buildings between 1908 and 1928. The 17-hectare farmstead was split off from the family farm and is run by the Seager Wheeler Historic Farm Society; it has been a national historic site since 1994.',
  'Wooden buildings with original layouts and finishes: the 1908 and 1925 houses, the barn, seed cleaning plant, chicken coop, blacksmith shop, ice house, outhouse and a 1918 garage, with shelterbelts, hedgerows and the remains of test plots, the first log house and a sod shanty (Canadian Register). The farm\'s page offers guided tours of the 1925 house.',
  'The farm where prairie wheat was bred to win the world.',
  'Run by the Seager Wheeler Historic Farm Society, 23 Seager Wheeler Road: Highway 312 east from Rosthern 6 km, north on Seager Wheeler Road 1 km, second entrance. Its page (read November 2026): closed for the 2026 season, reopening in June; 9 a.m. to 4 p.m., closed weekends except by appointment; adults $10, youth $5; guided tours by arrangement June to August (306-232-5959).',
  [W('Seager Wheeler\'s Maple Grove Farm'),HP('Seager Wheeler\'s Maple Grove Farm NHS',12136),L('Seager Wheeler Farm: tours and hours','https://www.seagerwheelerfarm.org/tours')],brick='no'),
N('doukhobor-dugout-house','Doukhobor Dugout House','Blaine Lake','sk','town','ruins',52.75421,-106.72392,'area','public','walk','','1899–1904',
  'Doukhobors fleeing persecution in Russia reached this ravine above the North Saskatchewan late in 1899 and dug a winter home into the slope by a spring, closing the hollow with three walls of dovetailed, pegged logs under a sod roof. It was lived in until 1904, when a permanent house was built nearby. A University of Saskatchewan dig in 2004 drew attention to it; it became a provincial heritage property in 2005 and is also a national historic site.',
  'The log timbers, dovetailed joints, dowel pins, door hinges and window frames of the dugout set into the ravine, and the spring (Canadian Register). The Town of Borden\'s page describes a self-guided walk of ten stops (the spring, a prayer home, a banya, two dugout houses, a museum and later buildings) and calls the dugout 436 feet long and home to nine families, which the register does not say: not confirmed.',
  'A winter house dug into a ravine by Doukhobors fleeing the Tsar.',
  'A quarter section about 8 km southeast of Blaine Lake, off Highway 12, run by volunteers (the Martensville Messenger, 2021, names the founder). The Town of Borden\'s page gives 2026 opening as select Saturdays in July and August, 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. (adults $14); at other times the land is closed: ask the operators (doukhobordugouthouse.com).',
  [W('Doukhobor Dugout House'),HP('Doukhobor Dugout House',10428),L('Town of Borden: Doukhobor Dugout House','https://bordensask.ca/doukhobor-dugout-house'),L('Martensville Messenger: site reopening (2021)','https://martensvillemessenger.ca/doukhobor-dugout-site-opening-to-the-public-once-again-this-summer/')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
# ---- Saskatoon ----
N('marr-residence','Marr Residence','Saskatoon','sk','civic','standing',52.11863,-106.66327,'exact','ask','drive','','1883–1884',
  'A wood-frame house built in 1883–84 for the stonemason Alexander "Sandy" Marr in the Temperance Colony that became Saskatoon, believed to be from the first barge load of lumber sent to the colony. Middleton\'s forces took it as a field hospital in 1885. It stayed a home until the late 1970s, was bought by the City in 1979, and is the oldest building in Saskatoon on its original site; a national historic site since 2016.',
  'A 1½-storey house with a Mansard roof, dormers and verandah; inside, the great room, two original bedrooms and the split staircase, with lathing, plaster and drop siding of 1885 (City heritage register). Restored to the 1880s with displays on the field hospital (Museums Association).',
  'Saskatoon\'s oldest house on its first site, a hospital in 1885.',
  'City-owned, run by a volunteer board, 326 11th Street East (306-652-1201). The Museums Association listing: open for programs, tours and workshops through the year, not daily; by appointment. The listing notes the Victoria Bridge was closed; use the Broadway Bridge. Street parking.',
  [W('Marr Residence'),YXE('Marr Residence','marr-residence'),HP('326 - 11th Street East',4986),L('Museums Association of Saskatchewan: Marr Residence','https://saskmuseums.org/places/marr-residence/')],brick='no'),
N('little-stone-schoolhouse','Little Stone Schoolhouse (first Victoria School)','Saskatoon','sk','civic','standing',52.12944,-106.64083,'site','ask','walk','','1887–1888',
  'Saskatoon\'s first school and first public building, built of prairie fieldstone by Alexander Marr; the City says 1887, Wikipedia 1888. It stood at Broadway and 11th Street and was a meeting place for church and social life until a larger school replaced it. In 1911 it was taken down stone by stone, each stone numbered, and rebuilt on the new university campus, one of the first heritage projects in western Canada; it was restored and opened as a school museum in 1967.',
  'A one-room granite (fieldstone) school with a hip roof, east of St. Andrew\'s College on the University of Saskatchewan campus (City heritage register). Interior not checked.',
  'A school moved stone by numbered stone in 1911.',
  'Run by the university\'s Diefenbaker Canada Centre: open only for programs, booked tours and events, with open houses in July and August. The outside can be seen from the campus walks. Campus parking is paid; rules not checked.',
  [W('Victoria School (Saskatoon)'),YXE('Little Stone School House','little-stone-school-house'),L('Diefenbaker Canada Centre: Little Stone Schoolhouse','https://diefenbaker.usask.ca/visit/little-stone-schoolhouse.php')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('college-building-usask','College Building (Peter MacKinnon Building), University of Saskatchewan','Saskatoon','sk','inst','standing',52.13065,-106.6326,'exact','view','walk','','1910–1912',
  'The first building on the University of Saskatchewan campus: Sir Wilfrid Laurier laid its cornerstone on July 29, 1910, and it was built to 1912 as the College of Agriculture, to designs by Brown and Vallance of Montreal. It heads The Bowl, the green round which the first Collegiate Gothic buildings stand, built of greystone quarried just north of the campus. It was added to in 1985 and 2002 and is now named the Peter MacKinnon Building; a national historic site.',
  'Rock-faced grey fieldstone dressed with Bedford sandstone, gargoyles, a notched parapet and oriel windows; inside, oak doors, the arched ceiling and tiled floor of the main corridor, a 2½-storey assembly hall with stage and balcony, and a terra cotta frieze to the staff and students killed in the First World War (Canadian Register).',
  'The greystone heart of the prairie\'s first university.',
  'University of Saskatchewan (university administration). The outside is on the open campus; when the public may go inside was not checked.',
  [W('Peter MacKinnon Building'),HP('College Building',6772)],brick='no'),
N('saskatoon-cpr-station','Saskatoon Railway Station (Canadian Pacific)','Saskatoon','sk','rail','standing',52.13258,-106.67064,'exact','view','drive','stations','1907–1908',
  'A two-storey Chateau-style station built by the Canadian Pacific in 1907–08 and enlarged in 1919, when Saskatoon was the meeting point of three railways. It was a passenger, telegraph, mail and freight depot until passenger service ended in 1960, and CP offices until 1993, when it was sold and restored. A national historic site since 1976 and a protected heritage railway station since 1990.',
  'Steep hipped roofs and a polygonal tower, yellow face brick trimmed with white Tyndall stone, cedar-shingle roofs, bracketed eaves and original wood window frames; some original partitions, finishes and transom lights inside (Canadian Register).',
  'The CPR\'s chateau in the Hub City.',
  'Privately owned, 305 Idylwyld Drive North; it now holds offices and businesses (Wikipedia; the register says a restaurant and offices). See it from the street; inside only as a customer of the businesses.',
  [W('Saskatoon station (Canadian Pacific Railway)'),HP('Saskatoon Railway Station (Canadian Pacific)',6773)],brick='no'),
N('bessborough-hotel','Bessborough Hotel','Saskatoon','sk','hotel','standing',52.12609,-106.65897,'exact','public','walk','','1930–1935',
  'A ten-storey Châteauesque hotel built by the Canadian National Railway on the riverbank, designed by J.S. Archibald and John Schofield. Work began in 1930 and the shell was up in 1931, but the Depression left it empty for four years; it opened on December 10, 1935 and was the tallest building in Saskatoon until 1966. It is one of the country\'s railway hotels and is run today as a Delta (Marriott) hotel.',
  'Red-orange brick walls under steep green-grey roofs and dormers, with pale stone trim (the photograph Wikipedia carries). Built of Canadian materials: bricks from Dominion Firebrick at Claybank, Saskatchewan, Tyndall stone, Estevan tiles and Montreal bronze. The City register describes the original interior (travertine stairs, terrazzo lobby floor, beamed and gold-leaf ceiling, a ballroom with ornate ceilings and chandeliers); how much of it survives was not checked. The river gardens face Kiwanis Park.',
  'The railway castle that stood empty through the Depression.',
  'A working hotel, 601 Spadina Crescent East, privately owned and managed by Delta Hotels; the riverbank Kiwanis Park on both sides is public. It has only a heritage holding bylaw (60-day delay on demolition), not full designation.',
  [W('Delta Bessborough'),YXE('Bessborough Hotel','bessborough-hotel')],brick='yes'),
N('nutana-pioneer-cemetery','Pioneer (Nutana) Cemetery','Saskatoon','sk','burial','standing',52.10052,-106.68334,'site','public','walk','cemeteries','1884–1948',
  'The Temperance Colony\'s first cemetery, on the high east bank of the South Saskatchewan; the first burial was Robert Clark, who died of pneumonia after fighting a prairie fire in 1884. For two decades it was the only cemetery; there were 162 known burials up to the last in 1948, about a third of them babies, and only 144 graves are identified. The bank slid in 1904, 1969 and 1984, damaging graves and forcing some to be moved.',
  'A 5.6-hectare grassed riverbank with a central cairn, old tombstones (Clark, Trounce, Fletcher and Caswell among the names), footpaths and a remnant of native prairie (City heritage register). The City asks visitors not to touch or lean on the markers.',
  'Saskatoon\'s first graves, slowly going into the river.',
  'City of Saskatoon, at Ruth Street West and St. Henry Avenue across from the Exhibition grounds, beside Diefenbaker Park. Hours not checked. Keep back from the eroding bank edge.',
  [W('Nutana Pioneer Cemetery'),YXE('Pioneer Cemetery','pioneer-cemetery'),L('City of Saskatoon: Nutana Cemetery (Pioneer Cemetery)','https://saskatoon.ca/community-culture-heritage/saskatoon-history-archives/nutana-cemetery-pioneer-cemetery')],th=[52.09952, -106.67979],thn='Lot on Ruth Street West (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no'),
N('next-of-kin-memorial-avenue','Next of Kin Memorial Avenue, Woodlawn Cemetery','Saskatoon','sk','burial','standing',52.14618,-106.65822,'site','public','walk','cemeteries','1922–1923',
  'After the First World War the Imperial Order Daughters of the Empire led the planting of a tree for each Saskatoon soldier killed, along a "Road of Remembrance" modelled on the tree-lined roads of France: 265 trees at first, each with a bronze plaque. The register calls it the only such avenue in Canada to keep its integrity. Woodlawn Cemetery itself was begun in 1905 as St. Paul\'s Roman Catholic Cemetery and taken over by the City in 1918.',
  'A 0.7 km avenue along the west side of the cemetery from a pair of stone pedestals and a wrought-iron fence to a stone cairn in a paved circle, lined by 112 elms with bronze plaques on iron stands; the cemetery holds more than 1,200 memorial trees in all (Canadian Register). Block 55 holds 107 Commonwealth war graves (Wikipedia).',
  'A road of elms, one for each soldier.',
  'City of Saskatoon\'s Woodlawn Cemetery, in the city\'s north end; the avenue begins at the south end. Cemetery hours not checked.',
  [W('Woodlawn Cemetery (Saskatoon)'),HP('Next of Kin Memorial Avenue NHS',13557),L('City of Saskatoon: Woodlawn Cemetery','https://www.saskatoon.ca/woodlawn')],brick='no'),
N('forestry-farm-saskatoon','Sutherland Forest Nursery Station (Forestry Farm Park)','Saskatoon','sk','industry','standing',52.157,-106.5842,'area','public','walk','','1913–1965',
  'The federal Forestry Branch opened a tree nursery and model farm at Sutherland in 1913; between then and 1966 it grew and shipped about 147 million caragana, ash, maple, elm and willow seedlings for prairie shelterbelts. It was run by the Prairie Farm Rehabilitation Administration in the drought years and closed in 1965, when the work moved to Indian Head. The City bought part of it in 1966; a zoo opened beside the old farmyard in 1972.',
  'The "farmyard" laid out like a progressive farm: the Superintendent\'s Residence in landscaped grounds, staff houses, tree packing and storage buildings, a pumphouse, greenhouse, blacksmith shop and barn, now used by the zoo, with shelterbelts and ornamental plantings around (Canadian Register). A path from the park entrance runs to the Superintendent\'s Residence (City), a two-storey red brick house with a hipped roof and a glassed porch in the photograph Wikipedia carries. Building interiors not checked.',
  'Where the trees of prairie shelterbelts were grown.',
  'City of Saskatoon park, open every day but December 25: 7:30 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. October to mid-April, to 9 p.m. May to Labour Day (last entry 30 minutes before). Parking $5 a day May to Labour Day; the zoo charges separately. No pets in the park.',
  [W('Forestry Farm Park and Zoo'),HP('Forestry Farm Park and Zoo NHS',7610),L('City of Saskatoon: Forestry Farm Park','https://saskatoon.ca/parks-recreation-attractions/events-attractions/saskatoon-forestry-farm-park-zoo/forestry-farm-park-0')],th=[52.15684, -106.58185],thn='Forestry Farm Park lot (OpenStreetMap)',brick='some'),
N('wanuskewin','Wanuskewin (Opimihaw valley archaeological sites)','Saskatoon','sk','landmark','traces',52.22616,-106.59313,'area','public','walk','','',
  'About twenty archaeological sites in the deep coulees of Opimihaw (Tipperary) Creek where it meets the South Saskatchewan record nearly 6,000 years of Northern Plains peoples: buried camps over 4,000 years old, bison kills and a bison pound, drive lanes, tipi rings, cairns and a medicine wheel. It became a national historic site in 1986. Wanuskewin Heritage Park, a non-profit run with First Nations representation, interprets it.',
  'The register names a medicine wheel, tipi rings, bison jumps, a bison pound, drive lanes and stone cairns along the creek; more than 6 km of trails from the visitor centre run through the valley.',
  'Six thousand years of camps in one creek valley.',
  'Wanuskewin Heritage Park (non-profit), Penner Road, 5 minutes north of Saskatoon. Its page: open daily 9:30 a.m. to 5 p.m. (closed fall and winter statutory holidays and December 25 to January 1); 2026 adult admission $16, parking $5. No pets, bikes or drones on the valley trails.',
  [W('Wanuskewin Heritage Park'),HP('Wanuskewin NHS',15685),L('Wanuskewin: hours and admission','https://wanuskewin.com/visit/hours-admission/'),L('Wanuskewin: trails','https://wanuskewin.com/experience/trails/')],th=[52.22335, -106.59773],thn='Wanuskewin visitor centre lot (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no'),
]
